  "limitations": [
    "The read edges are14,22,30,54,62,70ns. The read clock remains low from34to54ns; the last-read location is overwritten through the other port at50ns. The old read output persists until the new read edge. This is a24ns edge interval, not continuous125MHztraffic for the entire run.",
    "Measured input edge values reconstruct memory independently of the original runner q summaries;120stable-window samples and392stopped-clock samples pass. Five corrupted-wave controls are rejected.",
    "The previous model with corrected write addresses still used an asynchronous mem[latched_address] output. It changes when another port writes the last-read location while this read clock is stopped; both the transistor waveform and native reference retain their output. The new research model registers read outputs at their own clock edges.",
    "Native comparison uses the actual Ethernet technology map with only top/module-type renaming. It passes18439observations across all256addresses, full-word writes, read-enable holds, stopped read-clock overwrites and simultaneous accesses to different addresses.",
    "Transparent-old-output, stale-write-address, wrong-read-address and missing-hold variants all fail the same native comparison. BIST, arbitrary dual-writer use and same-address simultaneous collisions remain unsupported/unqualified.",
    "The TT circuit run supports these specific read/write observations; it does not prove arbitrary-input transistor/behavioral equivalence or setup/hold/read margins. SS/FF and half-step cases are still separate work and are deliberately excluded from this snapshot.",
    "No production RTL or macro mapping is changed. Clocked outputs plus wrapper held-state require real synthesized/physical timing and full-chip checks before adoption. Original full-chip LVS and final setup/slew gates remain open."
  ],
